WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.208 --> 00:00:02.728
- [Announcer] It's plastic;
it's most likely beige;

00:00:02.728 --> 00:00:04.200
and it sounds like this:

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.950
(recorder music)

00:00:08.404 --> 00:00:10.445
Chances are if you grew
up in the United States

00:00:10.445 --> 00:00:12.146
you've played a recorder.

00:00:12.146 --> 00:00:13.893
It was a non-negotiable part

00:00:13.893 --> 00:00:15.757
of your elementary school education,

00:00:15.757 --> 00:00:19.205
and for that, you can
thank this guy, Carl Orff,

00:00:19.205 --> 00:00:21.045
a passionate German composer.

00:00:21.045 --> 00:00:22.936
(dramatic classical music)

00:00:22.936 --> 00:00:25.517
Originally, the recorder was hand-crafted,

00:00:25.517 --> 00:00:28.546
wooden, and made for
the highest of society.

00:00:28.546 --> 00:00:32.125
Even Vivaldi and Bach wrote
pieces for the recorder.

00:00:32.125 --> 00:00:35.098
It doesn't rely on a reed
or strings, just breath.

00:00:35.098 --> 00:00:36.386
(recorder playing)

00:00:36.386 --> 00:00:37.648
It's in the flute family.

00:00:37.648 --> 00:00:40.077
In the 1960s, the recorder
started being produced

00:00:40.077 --> 00:00:42.752
out of plastic — cheap plastic.

00:00:42.752 --> 00:00:44.425
(recorder playing)

00:00:44.425 --> 00:00:46.083
So, how did it become the clumsy,

00:00:46.083 --> 00:00:48.595
awkward sound, we all used to play?

00:00:48.595 --> 00:00:51.242
(recorders playing)

00:00:51.242 --> 00:00:52.864
That's where Orff comes in.

00:00:52.864 --> 00:00:54.854
He saw the recorder as an easy way

00:00:54.854 --> 00:00:56.921
to get kids to start playing music.

00:00:56.921 --> 00:00:58.064
The logic was simple.

00:00:58.064 --> 00:01:00.944
The recorder relies on rhythm
rather than memorization.

00:01:00.944 --> 00:01:03.644
If you can sing, you
most likely can play it.

00:01:03.644 --> 00:01:04.934
(recorders playing)

00:01:04.934 --> 00:01:06.624
Orff had the best of intentions,

00:01:06.624 --> 00:01:08.854
to inspire the next
generation of musicians,

00:01:08.854 --> 00:01:11.715
and even though they can
sometimes be annoying,

00:01:11.715 --> 00:01:13.246
our hats are off to you, sir,

00:01:13.246 --> 00:01:15.384
for changing the course of music education

00:01:15.384 --> 00:01:17.033
for generations to come.

00:01:17.033 --> 00:01:20.033
(recorders playing)

